    "narrative": "The standout play for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is **Mickey Moniak**, who leads our starting nine with an elite 75.98 efficiency score. Despite the \"Lineup Pending\" tag, the projection factors in a massive +14% boost from a favorable platoon advantage and park factors, alongside a +2% edge against the opposing pitcher\u2019s slow delivery, which provides a high-upside opportunity for stolen bases\u2014our scarcest category. **Isaac Paredes** (64.31) and **Josh Naylor** (60.48) also anchor the middle of our order as high-floor, high-production targets; **Isaac Paredes** specifically benefits from a strong platoon profile and a manageable 4.49 xERA matchup, making him a primary candidate to drive in runs tonight.\n\nThe bench decision process was tightest around **Austin Riley**, who yielded a 54.15 score but ultimately failed to crack the starting rotation. While **Austin Riley** offers a boost for stolen base potential due to the opposing pitcher\u2019s slow delivery, his projected efficiency fell just short of our utility and corner infield options, particularly because his projected #8 spot in the order cannibalizes his RBI floor. We are keeping a close eye on **Corbin Carroll**, who at 59.23 remains a must-start despite facing a tough matchup against Tolle. While the stolen base potential is dampened by an elite holding pitcher (50% SB risk), **Corbin Carroll** remains one of our most dynamic offensive catalysts.\n\nRisk management remains a priority given several challenging matchups. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (56.76) and **Travis Bazzana** (41.79) are both facing left-handed starters, suppressing their ceilings due to platoon splits; **Travis Bazzana** is particularly close to our 40.0 \"Below Floor\" threshold and will need to be monitored closely during the game. We have opted to sit **Zach McKinstry** (34.56) entirely today, as the combination of an elite matchup against Skenes and our strict efficiency floor dictates that we preserve our remaining game caps for more favorable windows.",
